{
  "items": [
    {
      "format": "essay",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "context": "A research team studies a fictional enzyme, E, that converts substrate S into product. The team measures the initial rate of product formation under different conditions. Unless noted otherwise, each reaction contains the same amount of enzyme, and each variable is tested while the others are held constant.\n\nTemperature was tested at pH 7.0 with no inhibitor.\n\n| Temperature (°C) | Initial rate (µmol/min) |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| 5 | 8 |\n| 20 | 35 |\n| 37 | 80 |\n| 50 | 42 |\n| 65 | 2 |\n\npH was tested at 37°C with no inhibitor.\n\n| pH | Initial rate (µmol/min) |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| 3 | 3 |\n| 5 | 20 |\n| 7 | 80 |\n| 9 | 25 |\n| 11 | 1 |\n\nTo test an inhibitor, the team measured rates at 37°C and pH 7.0 at several substrate concentrations.\n\n| Substrate concentration (mM) | Rate without inhibitor (µmol/min) | Rate with inhibitor (µmol/min) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 10 | 5 |\n| 4 | 29 | 15 |\n| 16 | 62 | 31 |\n| 64 | 77 | 39 |",
      "question": "Answer all parts.\n\n(a) Identify the temperature and pH at which enzyme E has its highest measured activity.\n\n(b) Describe how the rate changes as temperature and pH move away from the conditions that produce the highest rate. Explain how changes in molecular motion or enzyme structure can account for these patterns.\n\n(c) The inhibitor is reversible. Determine whether the results are more consistent with competitive or noncompetitive inhibition. Justify your answer using the rates at different substrate concentrations, and explain how this type of inhibitor affects enzyme activity.\n\n(d) Propose a follow-up experiment to test whether the very low rate at 65°C results from a persistent change in enzyme structure or from the lower activity measured while the enzyme is at that temperature. State the comparison you would make and the results that would support a persistent structural change.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "criterion": "Part (a): identifies the temperature optimum",
          "description": "Identifies 37°C as the tested temperature with the highest rate.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (a): identifies the pH optimum",
          "description": "Identifies pH 7 as the tested pH with the highest rate.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (b): explains the temperature pattern",
          "description": "Explains that lower temperatures reduce molecular motion and the frequency of effective enzyme-substrate interactions, while temperatures above the optimum can disrupt interactions that maintain enzyme structure and alter the active site.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (b): explains the pH pattern",
          "description": "Explains that pH changes can alter charges and interactions in the enzyme, changing its structure or active site and reducing activity away from the optimum.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (c): identifies the inhibition pattern",
          "description": "Identifies the results as more consistent with noncompetitive inhibition.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (c): supports the inference and explains the effect",
          "description": "Uses the data to note that the inhibitor continues to reduce the rate substantially at high substrate concentration, and explains that a noncompetitive inhibitor binds away from the active site and reduces activity by changing enzyme function or structure.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (d): proposes a useful comparison",
          "description": "Proposes incubating one enzyme sample at 65°C, returning it to 37°C before measuring its rate, and comparing it with an equal enzyme sample kept at 37°C throughout.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (d): states an interpretable prediction",
          "description": "States that persistently lower activity in the heated sample after it returns to 37°C would support a lasting structural change, whereas recovery toward the control rate would support a temperature effect that is not persistent.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ],
      "keyPoints": [
        "The highest measured rate occurs at 37°C and pH 7.",
        "Lower temperature reduces molecular motion and effective collisions; excessive heat can disrupt interactions that maintain the enzyme's shape.",
        "pH changes can alter charges and interactions that help maintain the enzyme's structure and active site.",
        "The inhibitor's effect remains substantial at high substrate concentration, consistent with noncompetitive inhibition.",
        "Testing enzyme activity after heating and returning the sample to 37°C can distinguish a persistent effect from an effect limited to the high-temperature measurement."
      ],
      "sampleOutline": "The highest rates occur at 37°C and pH 7. At lower temperatures, reduced molecular motion lowers the frequency of effective interactions; high temperatures and pH values away from 7 can alter the enzyme's structure and active site. The inhibitor's effect remains substantial even at high substrate concentration, which is consistent with noncompetitive inhibition that reduces enzyme activity by binding away from the active site. To test the high-temperature effect, heat one sample at 65°C, return it to 37°C, and measure its rate alongside a sample kept at 37°C. A lasting reduction in the heated sample would support a persistent structural change."
    }
  ]
}

